# Line endings.
#
# Git for Windows ships with core.autocrlf=true, which rewrites checked-out
# files to CRLF. Every file here is either run inside a Linux container or read
# by a tool that treats a carriage return as part of the value, so that rewrite
# breaks things in ways that are genuinely hard to diagnose:
#
#   *.sh      run under Git Bash: `$'\r': command not found` on the first line
#   .env      compose reads `PUBLIC_URL=https://host\r` and the \r ends up in
#             the cookie domain, the tunnel hostname and every redirect
#   *.yml     a trailing \r inside a compose command string is passed to the
#             process as an argument
#   Dockerfile / *.mjs  copied into a Linux image and executed there
#
# Forcing LF on the way in and out means a Windows clone produces byte-identical
# files to a Linux one.
* text=auto eol=lf

*.sh text eol=lf
*.mjs text eol=lf
*.mts text eol=lf
*.ts text eol=lf
*.tsx text eol=lf
*.yml text eol=lf
*.yaml text eol=lf
*.json text eol=lf
*.md text eol=lf
*.css text eol=lf
Dockerfile text eol=lf
.env.example text eol=lf

# Windows-only helpers are the exception: PowerShell copes with LF, but a
# native editor on that box will fight anything else.
*.ps1 text eol=crlf

*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.ico binary
